WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:05.970
This is Anna. She just graduated from
college in the United States. And this is

00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:12.389
Sophia. She also just graduated from
college in Finland. Anna and Sophia both

00:00:12.389 --> 00:00:16.770
want to be middle school teachers. But it
turns out, there's a good chance their

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:23.189
experiences will be very different. So
different that Anna is twice as likely

00:00:23.189 --> 00:00:29.880
as Sophia to leave teaching for good.
That's causing a problem. The supply of

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:34.320
new certified teachers in the United
States is shrinking, but the number of

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:39.690
public school students keeps growing.
Massive teacher shortages. Warnings about

00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:44.969
teacher turnover. Educators call
Colorado's teacher shortage a crisis.

00:00:44.969 --> 00:00:51.300
So what makes Sophia stay and Anna leave? And how can the United States keep more

00:00:51.300 --> 00:01:00.690
of its teachers in the classroom? In the
US, teachers work about nine and a

00:01:00.690 --> 00:01:05.339
quarter hours a day. That's an hour and a
half longer than the average for

00:01:05.339 --> 00:01:10.220
teachers in other countries in the
Organization for Economic Development or OECD

00:01:10.220 --> 00:01:15.689
for short. That's a group of mostly
wealthy countries that economists often

00:01:15.689 --> 00:01:19.920
compare to one another. Teachers in the
US. work more than two and a half hours

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:25.650
longer than their colleagues in South
Korea, Finland, and Israel. There are some

00:01:25.650 --> 00:01:30.210
countries with similar teacher work
hours to the United States, like New

00:01:30.210 --> 00:01:36.689
Zealand, Singapore, and the UK. Teachers in Japan for example work nearly two hours

00:01:36.689 --> 00:01:42.299
more per day than teachers in the US, but
in all of these countries teaching hours

00:01:42.299 --> 00:01:46.170
are much lower. Of the nine and a quarter hours that

00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:50.100
American teachers work every day, they
spend about five and a half of those

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:55.979
hours actually teaching. That's more than
the OECD average and significantly more

00:01:55.979 --> 00:02:02.100
than teachers in New Zealand, the UK,
South Korea, Japan, and Singapore.

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:06.220
Teachers in these countries get more time for planning, grading, and collaborating with each other.

00:02:06.220 --> 00:02:10.600
So do all those extra teaching hours translate to better results?

00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:17.250
Students in the US score slightly above the OECD average on the

00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:22.230
PISA exam, which tests 15 year-olds all
over the world in reading, science, and

00:02:22.230 --> 00:02:27.180
math. But they score lower than students
in countries like Finland, South Korea,

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:31.624
Japan, and Singapore, where teaching hours are much lower.

00:02:31.624 --> 00:02:33.540
If we look inside Anna and Sofia's classrooms in the US and Finland,

00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:39.360
we'd see Anna teaching an hour and a half

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:44.489
more per day than Sofia. Anna also spends more time planning lessons, grading

00:02:44.489 --> 00:02:49.920
student work, and leading extracurricular
activities. But those extra hours aren't

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:52.902
necessarily reflected in Anna's paycheck.

00:02:52.902 --> 00:02:55.739
If you compare Sofia to other people in

00:02:55.739 --> 00:02:59.820
Finland with college degrees, she makes
about 98 cents for every dollar that

00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:04.019
they make. That's on par with the pay
ratio between teachers and college

00:03:04.019 --> 00:03:08.130
graduates in similar countries. But Anna
and other American middle school

00:03:08.130 --> 00:03:12.540
teachers only make about 65 cents for
every dollar that their college-educated

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:20.070
peers make. Still, as politicians in the
US never tire of pointing, out we spend

00:03:20.070 --> 00:03:24.000
more per student than almost any country
I think than nearly every other country

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:28.950
in the developed world. But that figure
varies a lot by state. New York spends

00:03:28.950 --> 00:03:33.299
twice as much as California on each
student. Mississippi spends less than

00:03:33.299 --> 00:03:38.700
half as much as Alaska. And American
schools generally spend a lot more on

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:43.170
security and other non-instructional
costs than schools in other countries.

00:03:43.170 --> 00:03:48.239
Plus, if you look at the share of its
national wealth or GDP that each country

00:03:48.239 --> 00:03:52.440
spends on education, you can see there
are plenty of countries spending a

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:58.000
bigger share than the US. There's one
other difference between Anna and Sofia.

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:03.210
When they're asked whether people in
their country value teachers, two out of

00:04:03.210 --> 00:04:09.680
three Finnish teachers say yes. But just
one in three American teachers agree.

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:14.970
There are a lot of reasons why teachers
like Anna leaves a classroom, but if the

00:04:14.970 --> 00:04:19.680
US wants to keep more of them around, we might want to take a few pages from

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:22.400
Finland's book.

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:32.220
you

